# Line endings.
#
# The repository is developed on Windows (core.autocrlf=true), so text files
# are checked out with CRLF. That is fine for TypeScript and Markdown, and
# the repository is full of them.
#
# It is NOT fine for the files that end up inside a Linux container:
#
#   * a shell script with CRLF endings has a shebang line reading
#     "#!/bin/sh\r", which the kernel cannot resolve — the container dies
#     with "no such file or directory" and blames the image;
#   * its line continuations break, so `cmd \` followed by an indented
#     pipeline becomes a syntax error;
#
# and the failure appears at deploy time on someone else's platform, where
# the obvious thing to look at is not the line ending.
#
# So these files are LF everywhere: in the repository, in a Windows checkout,
# and in the image.
* text=auto

*.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
.dockerignore text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitignore text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
